---
import PageMeta from "../components/PageMeta.astro";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../config";
---

<PageLayout>
  <PageMeta title={`Privacy | ${SITE_TITLE}`} slot="meta" />
  <div slot="main" class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Last updated: October 13, 2026</p>

    <p>
      This is a personal blog. It doesn't set any cookies of its own, doesn't show
      ads and doesn't sell or share data. Here's everything that touches
      your data when you visit.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel</a>.
      Like any web server, Vercel processes your IP address to deliver the
      pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      I use <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/privacy-policy">Vercel Web Analytics</a>
      and <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/privacy-policy">Speed Insights</a>
      to see which pages are read and how fast they load. They don't use
      cookies or store anything on your device. Visitors are counted using
      an anonymous hash that resets every day, so you can't be recognised
      across days or sites, and IP addresses aren't stored. The legal basis is
      my legitimate interest in understanding how the blog is used.
    </p>

    <h2>Comments</h2>
    <p>
      Comments use <a href="https://giscus.app">giscus</a>, which stores them
      in GitHub Discussions. The comments section loads from giscus.app, so
      giscus and GitHub receive your IP address when you open a post. giscus
      doesn't track you or use analytics; see its
      <a href="https://github.com/giscus/giscus/blob/main/PRIVACY-POLICY.md">privacy policy</a>.
      If you sign in to comment, a session token is saved in your browser's
      local storage so you stay signed in. Comments are public and are stored
      by GitHub under your GitHub account.
    </p>

    <h2>Embedded content</h2>
    <p>
      Some posts embed videos (YouTube), tweets (X) and code (CodePen,
      GitHub Gist). These services receive your IP address and may set
      their own cookies, so they only load if you accept in the cookie
      banner. If you reject, each one shows a <em>Load</em> button instead,
      so you can still load a single embed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <button
        type="button"
        id="reset-consent"
        class="rounded bg-black px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-bold text-white dark:bg-white dark:text-black"
        >Change cookie choice</button
      >
    </p>

    <h2>Stored on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Your light/dark theme choice is kept in session storage, which is
      cleared when you close the tab. Your accept or reject choice for
      embedded content is kept in local storage so the banner doesn't ask
      again.
      Anything else is set by the third-party services described above, not
      by this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights and contact</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you can ask what data I hold about you, and ask me to
      correct or delete it. Since I don't collect personal data myself,
      there's usually nothing to hand over, but feel free to get in touch:
      <a href="mailto:safehiana@gmail.com">safehiana@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<script>
  import { setConsent } from "../lib/consent";

  document
    .getElementById("reset-consent")
    ?.addEventListener("click", () => setConsent(null));
</script>
